peScript Lesson 17: Utility Types 🛠️
Aaj hum 4 important Utility Types seekhenge: Partial, Pick, Omit aur Record.
Simple words mein, Utility Types ki help se hum existing type ko reuse karke uska naya version bana sakte hain. Har baar poora type dobara likhne ki zaroorat nahi hoti.
1. Partial<T> — sab properties optional
Maan lo ek user ka type hai:
type User = {
    name: string;
    age: number;
    email: string;
};


Normally, object mein teeno properties deni padengi. Lekin agar user ki sirf age update karni hai, to kya karein?
Partial<User> use karo:
type User = {
    name: string;
    age: number;
    email: string;
};

function updateUser(data: Partial<User>): void {
    console.log(data);
}

updateUser({ age: 25 }); 
updateUser({ name: "Rahul" });


Dono valid hain, kyunki Partial<User> sab properties ko optional bana deta hai.
Yaad rakho: Partial = saari properties optional.
2. Pick<T, K> — sirf selected properties
Maan lo tumhare paas user ki saari details hain, lekin tumhe sirf name aur email chahiye.
type User = {
    name: string;
    age: number;
    email: string;
};

type UserPreview = Pick<User, "name" | "email">;

const user: UserPreview = {
    name: "Rahul",
    email: "rahul@gmail.com"
};


UserPreview mein age required nahi hai, kyunki humne use select nahi kiya.
Yaad rakho: Pick = jo properties chahiye, unhe select karo.
3. Omit<T, K> — selected properties hatao
Maan lo user ka password database mein hai, lekin profile display karte waqt password nahi chahiye.
type User = {
    name: string;
    email: string;
    password: string;
};

type PublicUser = Omit<User, "password">;

const user: PublicUser = {
    name: "Rahul",
    email: "rahul@gmail.com"
};


Ab PublicUser mein name aur email hain, lekin password nahi hai.
Yaad rakho: Omit = selected properties ko hatao.
Note: Type se password hatana sirf type-level protection hai. Actual API response se sensitive data hatana bhi zaroori hai.
4. Record<K, V> — keys aur values ka structure
Maan lo har subject ke marks store karne hain.
type Subject = "Maths" | "Science" | "English";

const marks: Record<Subject, number> = {
    Maths: 90,
    Science: 85,
    English: 88
};

console.log(marks.Maths);


Output:
90


Yahan:
- Subject allowed keys define karta hai.
- number har key ki value ka type define karta hai.
TypeScript check karega ki required keys present hon aur unki values numbers hon.
Yaad rakho: Record = keys aur unki values ka type define karo.

Quick revision
Utility Type	Kaam
Partial<T>	Sab properties optional
Pick<T, K>	Selected properties rakho
Omit<T, K>	Selected properties hatao
Record<K, V>	Keys aur values ka structure
